{"id":"not-hired-again","title":"Not given a new contract after an overrun","question":"Which contractors whose agreement grew by half or ran two years long were not given another contract by the same department?","finding":"Of 125 contractor-and-department pairs whose agreement crossed that line at least two years ago, 44 (35%) show no new contract from the same department since. 26 of those 44 were still being paid by that department in fiscal 2026 or later, usually under the same agreement, which runs on; 11 hold newer contracts with other departments. The files give no reason in any case: no record on disk says a contractor was passed over because an agreement grew.","how":"An overrun here is a Board-approved agreement whose ceiling reached half again its first amount, or whose end date moved two years or more past the first; the date it crossed that line is the date the Board passed the amendment that did it. One row per contractor and department (the largest rise where there are several), only where at least a year has passed since and the name in the Board title is a supplier name in the contract table (a title that shortens or lengthens a name is matched to the one supplier whose name begins or ends with it). New contracts are rows of the Supplier Contracts table for the same contractor and the same department with a start date after that day and a ceiling above zero, leaving out the grown agreement itself. \"Competed\" and \"not competed\" come from the purchasing rule keyed on each new contract (emergency, sole source, bidding waived by ordinance and other \"not bid\" rules count as not competed; rules that do not say are \"unclear\"). Where a Board title names no department, new contracts from any department are counted. This is the other side of that screen: pairs with no new contract, where at least two years have passed. \"Last paid\" is the last fiscal year with payments from that department under any contract; \"runs to\" is the latest end date in the Board titles.","limits":"Not being given a new contract is not a penalty. A single long agreement can simply still be running, the work can have ended (pandemic testing, hotel rooms and meals are examples), a firm can have been merged or renamed so that its later contracts sit under another name, and names are matched by spelling. A one-off purchase of buses or equipment is not followed by another. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"contractor","t":"Contractor","type":"org"},{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"what","t":"Agreement that grew","type":"text"},{"k":"kind","t":"Kind of work","type":"text"},{"k":"over","t":"Over the first ceiling","type":"pct"},{"k":"monthsOver","t":"Months past the first end","type":"int"},{"k":"crossed","t":"Crossed the line on","type":"date"},{"k":"runsTo","t":"Agreement runs to","type":"date"},{"k":"lastPaid","t":"Last fiscal year paid by the department","type":"year"},{"k":"stillPaid","t":"Still paid in fiscal 2026 or later","type":"text"},{"k":"elsewhere","t":"Newer contracts with other departments","type":"int"},{"k":"reads","t":"What the growth looks like","type":"text"},{"k":"link","t":"Board file","type":"link"}],"rows":[{"department":"Public Works","what":"Traffic Company and Forensic Services Division Facility - Earthquake Safety and Emergency ","kind":"professional services","over":10.934,"crossed":"2016-02-10","reads":"same term, higher ceiling","link":{"t":"Board file 151216","u":"https://sfgov.legistar.com/LegislationDetail.aspx?ID=2523602&GUID=4B29D1CE-9067-4B94-9F58-D0714D095BF9"},"lastPaid":2023,"elsewhere":0,"stillPaid":"no","contractor":"Hellmuth Obata + Kassabaum Inc","contractorKey":null},{"department":"Human Services Agency","what":"Great Plates Delivered","kind":"other services","over":3.89,"monthsOver":12,"crossed":"2020-12-1